==English==
{{was wotd|2026|September|7}}
[[File:Bang.svg|thumb|100px|An [[exclamation mark]], formerly also known as an exclamation ''({{senseno|en|punctuation mark}})''.]]

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:exclamacioun<ety:bor<frm:exclamation<ety:lbor<la:exclāmātiō>>>>|tree=1}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm|exclamacion,exclamacioun|t=calling or crying out; complaint, lament}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=exclamāciǒun|pos=n|id=MED14827}}</ref> borrowed from {{der|en|frm|exclamation}} (modern {{cog|fr|exclamation}}), from {{der|en|la|exclāmātiō|t=exclamation}}, from {{m|la|exclāmō|t=to call out, cry; to exclaim, shout}}<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=4669071913|date=June 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> + {{m|la|-tiō|pos={{lg|suffix}} forming [[action noun]]s denoting processes, actions, or results of actions}}. {{m|la||Exclāmō}} is derived from {{m|la|ex-|pos={{lg|prefix}} meaning ‘away; out’}} + {{m|la|clāmō|t=to call out, cry; to exclaim, shout}} (probably ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kelh₁-|t=to call out to}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɛkskləˈmeɪʃn̩/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɛkskləˈmeɪʃən/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-exclamation.wav|a=GA}}{{audio|en|En-us-exclamation.ogg|-}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=4}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ex|clam|a|tion}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[act#Noun|act]] of [[loudly]] [[call#Verb|calling]] or [[cry out|crying out]], for example, as in [[anger#Noun|anger]], [[grief#Noun|grief]] or [[pain#Noun|pain]], [[joy#Noun|joy]], [[surprise#Noun|surprise]], etc.; {{lb|en|countable}} an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this.
#* {{RQ:Fabyan Cronycle|year=1533|volume=I|chapter=CCVII|folio=cxxxi|column=1|passage=And when he {{quote-gloss|{{w|William the Conqueror}}}} was borne of his moders {{quote-gloss|{{w|Herleva}}'s}} wombe&#11805; he fyll to the groũde {{quote-gloss|grounde}}, and cloſed his handes wyth powder of the flore or pauement. Therfore the mydwyfe made an '''exclamacyon''', and ſayd this chyld ſhall be a kynge.}}
#* {{RQ:T. More Workes|title=Poysoned Booke|book=IV|chapter=IIII|page=1106|column=1|passage={{quote-gloss|He}} prooueth himſelf willinglye and wittingly in all his high tragycall '''exclamacion''' againſte hys owne conſcience, and his owne verye knowledge, to belye me: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Grafton Chronicle|volume=II|chapter=[[w:Henry II of England|Henrie the Seconde]]|page=72|passage=And ſo approching neerer vnto him ſayde, he had ſpoken ynough againſt his awne head: wherevpon followed great '''exclamation''' and many threatning words.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3 Q1|act=IV|scene=iv|sig=&#91;I4&#93;|verso=1|sigend=K|page=81|passage=Either be patient, and intreat me faire, / Or vvith the clamorus report of vvar: / Thus vvill I drovvne your '''exclamations'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|page=106|passage=[T]he Horror of dying in ſuch a miſerable Condition rais'd Vapours into my Head vvith the meer Apprehenſions; and in theſe Hurries of my Soul, I knovv not vvhat my Tongue might expreſs: but it vvas rather '''Exclamation''', ſuch as, Lord! vvhat a miſerable Creature am I?}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=I|page=11|passage="What can be the meaning of that emphatic '''exclamation'''?" cried he {{quote-gloss|Mr. Bennet}}. "Do you consider the forms of introduction, and the stress that is laid on them, as nonsense? I cannot quite agree with you ''there''. What say you, [[w:Mary Bennet|Mary]]? For you are a young lady of deep reflection, I know, and read great books and make extracts."}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Canongate 2|volume=II|chapter=VII|page=202|passage=At last, like Portuguese Catholics when exhausted with entreating their saints, the crowd without had recourse to vituperative '''exclamations'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|volume=III|page=45|passage="And now, my dearest Lucy, collect yourself, for all depends upon our own resources." Such were the whispered '''exclamations''' with which Francesca cheered her trembling companion, whose courage was not heightened by the darkness and stillness around them as they proceeded on their hazardous enterprise.}}
#* {{RQ:Stowe Uncle Tom's Cabin|volume=I|chapter=Select Incident of Lawful Trade|page=184|passage=The woman's passionate '''exclamations''' collected a crowd around her, and the trader briefly explained to them the cause of the agitation.}}
#* {{RQ:Black Princess of Thule|chapter=Lochaber No More|page=17|passage=Mackenzie turned from him with a petulant '''exclamation''', and went along and down the hill rapidly, as he could hear voices in the darkness.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of loudly [[complain]]ing or [[protest#Verb|protesting]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this; a [[complaint]], an [[outcry#Noun|outcry]], a [[protest#Noun|protest]].
#* {{RQ:Fabyan Cronycle|year=1533|volume=I|chapter=CLXXXII|folio=cvii|column=2|passage=Charlys herynge this '''exclamacyon''' of hys ſubgettes, and cõſyderyng hys lacke of power to with ſtande that malyce of hys enemyes, was right penſyfe and heuy in hys herte&#11805; {{...}}|translation=Charles hearing this '''exclamation''' of his subjects, and considering his lack of power to withstand that malice of his enemies, was right pensive and heavy in his heart, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Guevara Berners Golden Boke|chapter=Of Dyuers Other Thinges that the Vilayne Sayd before the Senate|folio=55|passage=And ſo the myſerable perſone that came to complayn, retourneth complaynyng on you all, curſinge his cruell deſtenies, and maketh '''exclamatiõ''' {{quote-gloss|exclamation}} vppon the rightwiſe goddis.}}
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|book=V|chapter=Epistle Dedicatorie|page=221|passage=[W]hen the eares of the people are thus continually beaten vvith '''exclamations''' againſt abuſes in the Church, theſe tunes come alvvaies moſt acceptable to them vvhoſe odious and corrupt dealings in ſecular affaires both paſſe by that meane the more couertly, & vvhatſoeuer happen doe alſo the leaſt feele that ſcourge of vulgar imputation vvhich notvvithſtanding they moſt deſerue.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=I|scene=ii|page=208|column=1|passage=Theſe exactions / {{...}} they are / Moſt pertinent to th'hearing, and to beare 'em, / The Backe is Sacrifice to th'load; They ſay / They are deuis'd by you, er {{quote-gloss|ere}} elſe you ſuffer / Too hard an '''exclamation'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Plague Year|page=176|passage=[T]he poor People vvho ran a-broad in their Extremities, vvere often ill-uſed and driven back again into the Tovvn; and this cauſed infinite '''Exclamations''' and Out-cries againſt the Country Tovvns, and made the Clamour very popular.}}
#* {{RQ:J. Priestley Philosophical Necessity|page=24|passage=[Y]et if a man expreſs the leaſt indignation at ſuch nevv and unheard-of arrogance, and in an argument of ſuch high importance as this, vvhat '''''exclamation''''' and ''abuſe'' muſt he not expect?}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Kenilworth|volume=I|chapter=XI|pages=284–285|pageref=284|passage=At this last exchange of sarcasm, Dame Crank set up her throat, and began a horrible '''exclamation''' against Jack Hostler, under cover of which Tressilian and his attendant escaped into the house.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar|countable}} {{synonym of|en|interjection|t=a [[phrase#Noun|phrase]] or [[word#Noun|word]] with no [[particular#Adjective|particular]] [[grammatical]] [[relation]] to a [[sentence#Noun|sentence]] but which [[serve#Verb|serves]] to [[express#Verb|express]] [[emotion]] or as a [[filled pause]]}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1 April 1857|author=&#91;{{w|Herbert Spencer}}&#93;|title=Art. V.—Progress: Its Law and Cause {{quote-gloss|book review}}.|journal=[[w:The Westminster Review|The Westminster and Foreign Quarterly Review]]|location=London|publisher=Savill and Edwards,{{nb...|printers, Chandos Street, Covent Garden.}}|volume=XI|issue=II (New Series; volume LXVII, number CXXXII, overall)|page=456|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_westminster-review_1857-04-01_67_132/page/456/mode/1up|oclc=1367803660|passage=The lowest form of language is the '''exclamation''', by which an entire idea is vaguely conveyed through a single sound; as among the lower animals. That human language ever consisted solely of '''exclamations''', as so was strictly homogeneous in respect of its parts of speech, we have no evidence. But that language can be traced down to a form in which nouns and verbs are its only elements, is an established fact.}}
# {{senseid|en|punctuation mark}}{{lb|en|orthography|countable|archaic}} {{synonym of|en|exclamation mark|t=the [[punctuation mark]] "{{l|mul|!}}", generally [[use#Verb|used]] to [[denote]] [[excitement]], [[shock#Noun|shock]], or surprise}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|author=[[w:John Russell, Viscount Amberley|[John Russell,] Viscount Amberley]]|chapter=Holy Books, or Bibles|title=An Analysis of Religious Belief|location=London|publisher=[[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner & Co.]],{{nb...|Ludgate Hill.}}|volume=II|page=12|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ananalysisrelig00russgoog/page/12/mode/1up|oclc=932710|passage="In this year," he {{quote-gloss|{{w|James Legge}}}} remarks, "and in the 24th year, we have the record of eclipses in successive months. According to modern chronologists such a thing could not be; but ''perhaps it did occur in ancient times!''" Dr Legge has italicised the concluding words, and put an '''exclamation''' after them, as if they embodied a surprising absurdity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1894|month=February|author=[[w:William Dean Howells|W[illiam] D[ean] Howells]]|title=A Masterpiece of Diplomacy. Farce.|journal=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper’s New Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]],{{nb...|publishers, 327 to 335 Pearl Street, Franklin Square.}}|volume=LXXXVIII|issue=DXXV|page=372|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/harpersnew088various/page/372/mode/1up|oclc=924884025|passage={{quote-gloss|''Mrs. Roberts'': "}}Did you leave word for Dr. Williams to hurry?" / ''Roberts'': "Yes, I left a very urgent message on his slate. I—" / ''Mrs. Roberts'': "I hope you underlined it, Edward! You never ''will'' underline things, even the most important!" / ''Roberts'': "Well, I underlined this, my dear." / ''Mrs. Roberts'': "How many times? Three times?" / {{...}} / ''Roberts'': "Yes, I think so—" / ''Mrs. Roberts'': "And did you put an '''exclamation''' after it? Three?" / ''Roberts'': "I don't know—" / ''Mrs. Roberts'': "Oh, how ''could'' you be so careless, Edward? If you didn't put three '''exclamations''', you might as well not have gone. He'll just take his time to do it, and Robby may be in a collapse by the time he gets here. He's furious now. Listen!"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author=w:Theodore Low De Vinne|chapter=Punctuation|title=The Practice of Typography: Correct Composition: A Treatise on Spelling Abbreviations, the Compounding and Division of Words, the Proper Use of Figures and Numerals, Italic and Capital Letters, Notes, etc.{{nb...|with Observations on Punctuation and Proof-reading}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:The Century Company|The Century Co.]]|pages=281 and 283|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cu31924032372777/page/n292/mode/1up|oclc=1047821657|passage={{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/cu31924032372777/page/n292/mode/1up page 281]}} The '''exclamation''' marks a word or phrase intended to express great surprise or emotion. {{...}} Short ejaculations, whether of enthusiasm, as ''Bravo! Hurrah! Good!'' or of contempt, as ''Get out! Away!'' or of sorrow, as ''Alas! O!'' always call for the '''exclamation'''. {{...}} {{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/cu31924032372777/page/n294/mode/1up page 283]}} It is rated an error to put an '''exclamation''' after every vocative O. {{...}} The exclamation-point, properly used, gives force and point to language; improperly and profusely used, it degrades intended pathos and makes bombast.}}
# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} {{synonym of|en|ecphonesis}} (”{{lb|en|uncountable}} the [[make#Verb|making]] of an [[emotional#Adjective|emotional]] [[utterance]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this; an [[exclamatory]] phrase”); also, {{synonym of|en|epiphonema|nocap=1}} (“{{lb|en|countable}} an exclamatory phrase or [[reflection]] used to [[round off]] or [[summarize]] an [[argument#Noun|argument]] or [[discourse#Noun|discourse]]”).
#* {{RQ:Puttenham English Poesie|chapter=Of Figures Sententious, otherwise Called Rhetoricall|page=177|passage=The figure of '''exclamation''', I call him [''the outcrie''] becauſe it vtters our minde by all ſuch vvords as do ſhevv any extreme paſſion, vvhether it be by vvay of exclamation or crying out, admiration or vvondering, imprecation or curſing, obteſtation or taking God and the vvorld to vvitnes, or any ſuch like as declare an impotent affection, {{...}}|footer=The marginal note states: “''Ecphoniſis'', or the Outcry.”}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|uncountable}} ''Followed by'' '''[[of#Preposition|of]]''': the act of [[formally]] [[declare|declaring]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this; a [[declaration]], a [[proclamation]], a [[pronouncement]].
#* {{RQ:Marston Antonio's Revenge|scene=iv|sig=E2|verso=1|page=43|passage=But on the ſodaine ſtraight Ile ſtand amaz'd, / And fall in '''exclamations''' of thy vertues.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1631|author=Thomas Powell|chapter=For Benefices Abroad|title=Tom of All Trades, or The Plaine Path-way to Preferment.{{nb...|Being a Discovery of a Passage to Promotion in All Professions, Trades, Arts, and Mysteries. Found out by an Old Travailer in the Sea of Experience, amongst the Inchanted Islands of Ill Fortune. Now Published for Common Good.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[d:Q51584226|B[ernard] Alsop]] and [[d:Q51585460|T[homas] Fawcet]], for Benjamen Fisher,{{nb...|and are to bee sold at his shop at the signe of the Talbot in Aldersgate-street.}}|page=17|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_tom-of-all-trades-or-th_powell-thomas-londino-_1631/page/17/mode/1up|oclc=84762003|passage=[M]any Patrons are content to preſent, according to the approbation of the Pariſhioners upon their hearing, and allovving, and due '''exclamation''' of the integrity of the life of ſuch ſuitors, and no othervviſe, divers governors, and gradations of the lands of divers Hoſpitals, and ''Meſons de dien'' have like right of preſentation to Benefices, as have other bodies Incorporate.}}

