# An [[investigation]] of a [[corpse]] to determine the cause of [[death]].
#: {{syn|en|autopsy|PM}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2025 February 19|author=Mike Lewis|title=Tragedy at Moorgate|journal=RAIL|issue=1029|page=59, about the {{w|Moorgate tube crash}}|text=Nor had the '''post mortem''' on Newson's body revealed any illness or other physical condition that might have prevented him from applying the brake, although after four days in the oven-like heat of the wreckage, the body was so badly decomposed as to make any reliable '''post mortem''' results unlikely.}} 
# {{lb|en|figuratively|management}} Any investigation after the conclusion of an activity, particularly when said activity produces an unwanted outcome.
#: {{syn|en|debriefing|AAR}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/jgm394/weed-greening-out-whitey-vomit|work={{w|Vice (magazine)|VICE}}|passage=Grinspoon finishes with some sage advice on the best way to avoid greening out: "Keep the doses low and know yourself. If it happens to you, try to do a '''postmortem''' on what happened, like: 'Was there alcohol involved? Was there nicotine involved?' Was I overtired? Did I take five puffs when I usually take two puffs?'"|title=Why You Sometimes Feel Sick or Vomit After Smoking Weed|date=2022-04-20|author=Ross Buchanan|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260216123947/https://www.vice.com/en/article/weed-greening-out-whitey-vomit/}}
# {{lb|en|Britain|college slang|obsolete}} At [[Cambridge]], a second [[examination]] for those who were "[[pluck]]ed" or failed in the first.

