#: {{coordinate terms|en|ball|cake|hank}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Elynour Rummyng|lines=309–311|page=105|passage=Some for very nede / Layde downe a '''skeyne''' of threde, / And some a '''skeyne''' of yarne; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Garland|lines=798–800|page=393|passage=Reche me that '''skane''' of tewly sylk; / And, Wynde me that botowme of such an hew, / Grene, rede, tawny, whyte, blak, purpill, and blew.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=IV|scene=iii|page=224|column=1|passage=Brau'd in mine ovvne houſe vvith a '''skeine''' of thred: / Avvay thou Ragge, thou quantitie, thou remnant, / Or I ſhall ſo be-mete thee vvith thy yard, / As thou ſhalt thinke on prating vvhil'ſt thou liu'ſt: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida Q1|act=V|scene=i|page=79|passage=No vvhy art thou then exaſperate, thou idle immaterial '''skiene''' of ſleiue ſilke, thou greene ſacenet flap for a ſore eye, thou toſſell of a Prodigals purſe—thou {{...}}|translation=No, why art thou then exasperate, thou idle immaterial '''skein''' of sleeve silk, thou greene sarsenet flap for a sore eye, thou tassel of a prodigal's purse—thou {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Davenant Love and Honour|page=10|column=2|passage=VVhat is ſhe heire to? a braſſe thimble, and / A '''skeane''' of brovvn thread?}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Giving Alms|page=16|passage=Suppoſe novv a VVork-houſe for Employment of Poor Children, ſets them to ſpinning of VVorſted.—For every '''Skein''' of VVorſted theſe Poor Children Spin, there muſt be a '''Skein''' the leſs Spun by ſome poor Family or Perſon that ſpun it before; {{...}} 'tis only the tranſpoſing the Manufacture from ''Colcheſter'' to ''London'', and taking the Bread out of the Mouths of the Poor of ''Eſſex'' to put it into the Mouths of the Poor of ''Middleſex''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1798|author=John Ebers|entry=Knouel|title=The New and Complete Dictionary of the German and English Languages{{nb...|Composed Chiefly after the German Dictionaries of Mr. [Johann Christoph] Adelung and of Mr. [Christian Friedrich] Schwan. Every German Word being Rendered into Proper English and Thoroughly Enriched with Phrases and Terms of Arts and Sciences: A Work, which will be Useful and Even Indispensible, and therefore Welcome to All Such as have a Mind to Translate or Read the Works of Either of the Two Languages, Elaborated by John Ebers.}}|location=Leipzig, Saxony|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Breitkopf & Härtel|Brietkopf and Haertel]]|volume=!II (H–R of the German Alphabet Explained in English)|page=360|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_4-9IAAAAcAAJ/page/360/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=993929329|passage=''Knouel, der, oder Kna̔uel'', a Bottom or Clevv of Thread or of Yarn, a Hank, a '''Skain''' of Yarn or Thread.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1807|author=[[w:George Gregory (British writer)|G[eorge] Gregory]]|entry=ROPE-MAKING|title=A Dictionary of Arts and Sciences.{{nb...|[...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} [T. Gillet] for [[w:Richard Phillips (publisher)|Richard Phillips]],{{nb...|No. 6, Bridge-Street, Blackfriars.}}|volume=II|pages=602–603|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofarts02greg/page/603/mode/1up|oclc=1547526516|passage=The aim of the rope-maker is to unite the strings of a great number of fibres. {{...}} This is done by twisting or twining them together, which causes them to compress each other. {{...}} Consequently, if we pull at this twisted '''skain''', we shall not separate it by drawing one parcel out from among the rest, but the whole fibres will break; and if the distribution of the fibres has been very equable, the '''skain''' will be nearly of the same strength in every part.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Antiquary|volume=I|chapter=I|page=4|passage=[A] sharp-looking old dame, {{...}} inhabited a "laigh {{quote-gloss|low}} shop," ''anglicé'' {{quote-gloss|in English}}, a cellar, opening to the High-street by a strait and steep stair, at the bottom of which she sold tape, thread, needles, '''skeans''' of worsted, coarse linen cloth, and such feminine gear, to those who had the courage and skill to descend to the profundity of her dwelling, without falling headlong themselves, or throwing down any of the numerous articles which, piled on each side of the descent, indicated the profession of the trader below.}}
#* {{RQ:Wilde Dorian Gray|page=12|passage=“How horribly unjust of you!” cried Lord Henry, tilting his hat back and looking up at the little clouds that, like ravelled '''skeins''' of glossy white silk, were drifting across the hollowed turquoise of the summer sky.}}
#* {{RQ:Woolf Voyage Out|chapter=XV|page=245|passage=The embroidery, which was a matter for thought, the design being difficult and the colours wanting consideration, brought lapses into the dialogue when she seemed to be engrossed in her '''skeins''' of silk, or, with head a little drawn back and eyes narrowed, considered the effect of the whole.}}
#* {{RQ:Christie Mysterious Affair|year=1927|page=180|passage="He did have a beard, sir," replied Dorcas, smiling. "And well I know it, for he borrowed two '''skeins''' of my black wool to make it with! And I’m sure it looked wonderfully natural at a distance.{{nb...}}"}}
